          "message": {
            "markdown": "\`id serial PRIMARY KEY\` on \`customers\` is a 4-byte key with a separate sequence: it stops at 2147483647 (inserts then fail with SQLSTATE \`2200H\`), and moving to \`bigint\` later rewrites the whole table. Declare it \`id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY\`: 4 more bytes per row, never runs out, and the SQL-standard identity refuses hand-written ids. Not needed for a small lookup table that cannot reach 2 billion rows; suppress with that reason. (PG017, [evidence](https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-keys-and-types/examples/bad/test.sql#L29-L37))",
            "text": "\`id serial PRIMARY KEY\` on \`customers\` is a 4-byte key with a separate sequence: it stops at 2147483647 (inserts then fail with SQLSTATE \`2200H\`), and moving to \`bigint\` later rewrites the whole table. Declare it \`id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY\`: 4 more bytes per row, never runs out, and the SQL-standard identity refuses hand-written ids. Not needed for a small lookup table that cannot reach 2 billion rows; suppress with that reason. (PG017, evidence: https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-keys-and-types/examples/bad/test.sql#L29-L37)",
