          "message": {
            "markdown": "\`email varchar(20)\` on \`customers\` enforces only a maximum: it still accepts \`''\`, an explicit cast silently truncates to 20 characters, and shrinking the limit later rewrites the table. Declare it \`text\` with a named check, \`CONSTRAINT customers_email_len CHECK (char_length(email) BETWEEN 1 AND 20)\`, which states the real rule and can be changed with \`NOT VALID\` then \`VALIDATE\` and no rewrite. Not needed when the limit is a real business rule the team keeps deliberately, or for fixed-length codes from a standard (then prefer \`text\` with a regular-expression check). (PG021, [evidence](https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-keys-and-types/examples/bad/test.sql#L39-L52))",
            "text": "\`email varchar(20)\` on \`customers\` enforces only a maximum: it still accepts \`''\`, an explicit cast silently truncates to 20 characters, and shrinking the limit later rewrites the table. Declare it \`text\` with a named check, \`CONSTRAINT customers_email_len CHECK (char_length(email) BETWEEN 1 AND 20)\`, which states the real rule and can be changed with \`NOT VALID\` then \`VALIDATE\` and no rewrite. Not needed when the limit is a real business rule the team keeps deliberately, or for fixed-length codes from a standard (then prefer \`text\` with a regular-expression check). (PG021, evidence: https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-keys-and-types/examples/bad/test.sql#L39-L52)",
