          "message": {
            "markdown": "\`created_at timestamp\` on \`customers\` is a timestamp without time zone: PostgreSQL "silently ignore[s] any time zone indication" on input, so the same instant written from two sessions with different \`TimeZone\` settings is stored as two values (12 hours apart in the db-skills test). Declare it \`timestamptz\`: stored as UTC, same 8 bytes, converted on output to the session's zone. Not needed for a wall-clock value that is deliberately zone-free (a store's opening time, a recurring local alarm); suppress with that reason. (PG019, [evidence](https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-keys-and-types/examples/bad/test.sql#L54-L61))",
            "text": "\`created_at timestamp\` on \`customers\` is a timestamp without time zone: PostgreSQL "silently ignore[s] any time zone indication" on input, so the same instant written from two sessions with different \`TimeZone\` settings is stored as two values (12 hours apart in the db-skills test). Declare it \`timestamptz\`: stored as UTC, same 8 bytes, converted on output to the session's zone. Not needed for a wall-clock value that is deliberately zone-free (a store's opening time, a recurring local alarm); suppress with that reason. (PG019, evidence: https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-keys-and-types/examples/bad/test.sql#L54-L61)",
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "lurie-guard/v1": "9bc31fc1b14f87db8c9258034f2df3ea",
