      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/lurielabs/lurie-guard",
          "name": "lurie-guard",
          "rules": [
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error",
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "\`CREATE INDEX\` or \`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX\` without \`CONCURRENTLY\`, on a table that is not created in the same migration set (this file plus every other added migration file of the PR).",
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## What it detects

\`CREATE INDEX\` or \`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX\` without \`CONCURRENTLY\`, on a table that is not created in the same migration set (this file plus every other added migration file of the PR).

## Why it matters

A plain \`CREATE INDEX\` "locks out writes (but not reads) on the table until it's done" ([CREATE INDEX](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createindex.html)). The db-skills bad example shows \`pg_locks\` for the session running \`CREATE INDEX orders_created_at_idx ON orders (created_at)\`: it holds \`ShareLock\` on \`orders\` for the whole build, and \`SHARE\` conflicts with the \`ROW EXCLUSIVE\` lock every \`INSERT\`, \`UPDATE\` and \`DELETE\` needs. On a table with traffic, every write waits for as long as the build takes.

## The fix

\`\`\`sql
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_created_at_idx ON orders (created_at);
\`\`\`

Run it outside a transaction block (PG003 checks that). If the build fails, drop the invalid index with \`DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY IF EXISTS\` and build again; never retry with \`IF NOT EXISTS\` (PG016).

## When it does not apply

- The table is created in the same migration set (\`CREATE TABLE\` in this file or in another added migration file of the PR): nothing reads it yet, so the lock costs nothing. The detector tracks the created tables across the PR.
- The statement already says \`CONCURRENTLY\`.
- A partitioned parent table: \`CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY\` is not supported on the parent, so the documented way is to build the index concurrently on each partition and then create the parent index without \`CONCURRENTLY\`. The detector cannot see that a table is partitioned; suppress with a reason that says so.
- An empty table or an agreed maintenance window: suppress with a reason (\`-- lurie-guard: ignore PG001 reason="..."\`).",
                "text": " What it detects

CREATE INDEX or CREATE UNIQUE INDEX without CONCURRENTLY, on a table that is not created in the same migration set (this file plus every other added migration file of the PR).

 Why it matters

A plain CREATE INDEX "locks out writes (but not reads) on the table until it's done" ([CREATE INDEX](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createindex.html)). The db-skills bad example shows pg_locks for the session running CREATE INDEX orders_created_at_idx ON orders (created_at): it holds ShareLock on orders for the whole build, and SHARE conflicts with the ROW EXCLUSIVE lock every INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE needs. On a table with traffic, every write waits for as long as the build takes.

 The fix

sql
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_created_at_idx ON orders (created_at);


Run it outside a transaction block (PG003 checks that). If the build fails, drop the invalid index with DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY IF EXISTS and build again; never retry with IF NOT EXISTS (PG016).

 When it does not apply

- The table is created in the same migration set (CREATE TABLE in this file or in another added migration file of the PR): nothing reads it yet, so the lock costs nothing. The detector tracks the created tables across the PR.
- The statement already says CONCURRENTLY.
- A partitioned parent table: CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY is not supported on the parent, so the documented way is to build the index concurrently on each partition and then create the parent index without CONCURRENTLY. The detector cannot see that a table is partitioned; suppress with a reason that says so.
- An empty table or an agreed maintenance window: suppress with a reason (-- lurie-guard: ignore PG001 reason="...").",
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/lurielabs/lurie-guard/blob/main/rules/pg/PG001-create-index-not-concurrently/RULE.md",
              "id": "PG001",
              "name": "create-index-not-concurrently",
              "properties": {
                "skill": "db-skills/skills/pg-online-migrations",
                "tags": [
                  "postgresql",
                  "migration",
                ],
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PG001: \`CREATE INDEX\` without \`CONCURRENTLY\` on an existing table",
              },
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error",
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "\`DROP INDEX\` without \`CONCURRENTLY\`, where the index is not created in the same migration set (a \`CREATE INDEX\` or a \`UNIQUE\`/\`PRIMARY KEY\` constraint of that name in this file or in another added migration file of the PR).",
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## What it detects

\`DROP INDEX\` without \`CONCURRENTLY\`, where the index is not created in the same migration set (a \`CREATE INDEX\` or a \`UNIQUE\`/\`PRIMARY KEY\` constraint of that name in this file or in another added migration file of the PR).

## Why it matters

"A normal \`DROP INDEX\` acquires an exclusive lock on the table, blocking other accesses until the index drop can be completed" ([DROP INDEX](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-dropindex.html)). The db-skills bad example drops \`orders_created_at_idx\` inside a transaction and shows \`pg_locks\` for the session: \`AccessExclusiveLock\` on \`orders\`, the table, not only on the index. A plain \`SELECT count(*) FROM orders\` from another session with \`lock_timeout = 200ms\` fails with SQLSTATE \`55P03\` behind it. In production the drop itself first queues behind every running query on the table, and from then on every new query queues behind the drop.

## The fix

\`\`\`sql
DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_created_at_idx;
\`\`\`

\`DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY\` "waits until conflicting transactions have completed" instead of blocking them. It takes one index name per statement, cannot run inside a transaction block (PG003 checks that), and does not support \`CASCADE\`.

## When it does not apply

- The index is created in the same migration set (a failed build being retried, or an index the PR itself adds and removes): nothing uses it yet. The detector tracks index names created by \`CREATE INDEX\` and by \`UNIQUE\`/\`PRIMARY KEY\` constraints across the added files.
- The statement already says \`CONCURRENTLY\`.
- An index on a partitioned table: "indexes on partitioned tables cannot be dropped using this option". Suppress with a reason that says so.",
                "text": " What it detects

DROP INDEX without CONCURRENTLY, where the index is not created in the same migration set (a CREATE INDEX or a UNIQUE/PRIMARY KEY constraint of that name in this file or in another added migration file of the PR).

 Why it matters

"A normal DROP INDEX acquires an exclusive lock on the table, blocking other accesses until the index drop can be completed" ([DROP INDEX](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-dropindex.html)). The db-skills bad example drops orders_created_at_idx inside a transaction and shows pg_locks for the session: AccessExclusiveLock on orders, the table, not only on the index. A plain SELECT count() FROM orders from another session with lock_timeout = 200ms fails with SQLSTATE 55P03 behind it. In production the drop itself first queues behind every running query on the table, and from then on every new query queues behind the drop.

 The fix

sql
DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_created_at_idx;


