            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error",
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "\`ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN\` on an existing table with a volatile \`DEFAULT\` (\`clock_timestamp()\`, \`random()\`, \`gen_random_uuid()\`, \`uuidv7()\`, \`nextval()\`, a subquery, or any function not in the detector's list of built-in stable and immutable functions), a \`GENERATED ... STORED\` column, or a \`GENERATED ... AS IDENTITY\` column.",
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## What it detects

\`ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN\` on an existing table with a volatile \`DEFAULT\` (\`clock_timestamp()\`, \`random()\`, \`gen_random_uuid()\`, \`uuidv7()\`, \`nextval()\`, a subquery, or any function not in the detector's list of built-in stable and immutable functions), a \`GENERATED ... STORED\` column, or a \`GENERATED ... AS IDENTITY\` column.

## Why it matters

"Adding a column with a volatile \`DEFAULT\` (e.g., \`clock_timestamp()\`), a stored generated column, an identity column, or a column with a domain data type that has constraints will cause the entire table and its indexes to be rewritten" ([ALTER TABLE](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altertable.html)). The db-skills bad example adds \`created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT clock_timestamp()\` to \`orders\` (100,000 rows) and asserts that \`pg_relation_filenode('orders')\` and \`pg_relation_filenode('orders_pkey')\` both change: a full copy of the table and its indexes, under \`ACCESS EXCLUSIVE\`, which blocks reads and writes for the whole copy and "temporarily require[s] as much as double the disk space". A constant default is metadata-only: the good example adds \`status text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'new'\` and the filenode stays.

## The fix

\`\`\`sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN created_at timestamptz;
ALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN created_at SET DEFAULT clock_timestamp();  -- new rows only
-- backfill the existing rows in batches, each batch its own transaction
-- then, if the column must be NOT NULL: the validated-CHECK path (PG007)
\`\`\`

## When it does not apply

- The table is created in the same migration set.
- The default is a constant or a call to a stable or immutable built-in (\`now()\`, \`current_timestamp\`, \`lower(...)\`, \`coalesce(...)\`): PostgreSQL evaluates it once and stores it in the metadata, no rewrite.
- A user-defined function marked \`IMMUTABLE\` or \`STABLE\`: the detector cannot see function volatility from the diff, so it treats unknown functions as volatile; suppress with a reason that names the function's volatility.
- A domain type with constraints also rewrites the table, but the detector cannot see domain definitions; not detected.",
                "text": " What it detects

ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN on an existing table with a volatile DEFAULT (clock_timestamp(), random(), gen_random_uuid(), uuidv7(), nextval(), a subquery, or any function not in the detector's list of built-in stable and immutable functions), a GENERATED ... STORED column, or a GENERATED ... AS IDENTITY column.

 Why it matters

"Adding a column with a volatile DEFAULT (e.g., clock_timestamp()), a stored generated column, an identity column, or a column with a domain data type that has constraints will cause the entire table and its indexes to be rewritten" ([ALTER TABLE](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altertable.html)). The db-skills bad example adds created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT clock_timestamp() to orders (100,000 rows) and asserts that pg_relation_filenode('orders') and pg_relation_filenode('orders_pkey') both change: a full copy of the table and its indexes, under ACCESS EXCLUSIVE, which blocks reads and writes for the whole copy and "temporarily require[s] as much as double the disk space". A constant default is metadata-only: the good example adds status text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'new' and the filenode stays.

 The fix

sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN created_at timestamptz;
ALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN created_at SET DEFAULT clock_timestamp();  -- new rows only
-- backfill the existing rows in batches, each batch its own transaction
-- then, if the column must be NOT NULL: the validated-CHECK path (PG007)


 When it does not apply

- The table is created in the same migration set.
- The default is a constant or a call to a stable or immutable built-in (now(), current_timestamp, lower(...), coalesce(...)): PostgreSQL evaluates it once and stores it in the metadata, no rewrite.
- A user-defined function marked IMMUTABLE or STABLE: the detector cannot see function volatility from the diff, so it treats unknown functions as volatile; suppress with a reason that names the function's volatility.
- A domain type with constraints also rewrites the table, but the detector cannot see domain definitions; not detected.",
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/lurielabs/lurie-guard/blob/main/rules/pg/PG005-add-column-rewrites-table/RULE.md",
              "id": "PG005",
              "name": "add-column-rewrites-table",
              "properties": {
                "skill": "db-skills/skills/pg-online-migrations",
                "tags": [
                  "postgresql",
                  "migration",
                ],
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PG005: \`ADD COLUMN\` that rewrites the table",
              },
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error",
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "\`ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ... NOT NULL\` with no \`DEFAULT\`, no identity, no generated expression and not a \`serial\` type, on a table that is not created in the same migration set.",
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## What it detects

\`ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ... NOT NULL\` with no \`DEFAULT\`, no identity, no generated expression and not a \`serial\` type, on a table that is not created in the same migration set.

## Why it matters

Nothing gives the existing rows a value, so the statement cannot succeed on a table with rows. The db-skills bad example runs \`ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN sku text NOT NULL\` on the 100,000-row \`orders\` and asserts SQLSTATE \`23502\` (\`not_null_violation\`, "column contains null values"): the migration aborts at deploy time, after taking \`ACCESS EXCLUSIVE\` on the table to find that out. It passes on the empty database of the test suite, which is why it reaches review. With a constant default the column is metadata-only: "the default value is evaluated at the time of the statement and the result stored in the table's metadata" ([ALTER TABLE](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altertable.html)); the good example adds \`status text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'new'\` and the filenode stays.

## The fix

\`\`\`sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN sku text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'unknown';
\`\`\`

Or, when no constant makes sense: add it nullable, backfill in batches, then make it \`NOT NULL\` through a validated \`CHECK\` (PG007).

## When it does not apply

- The table is created in the same migration set.
- The column has a \`DEFAULT\` (a volatile one is PG005), is an identity column, a generated column or a \`serial\`: each gives every row a value (identity and generated rewrite the table, PG005).
- The column is a \`PRIMARY KEY\` in the same clause: the key fails for other reasons on a non-empty table (PG012 and PG017 apply).",
                "text": " What it detects

ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ... NOT NULL with no DEFAULT, no identity, no generated expression and not a serial type, on a table that is not created in the same migration set.

 Why it matters

Nothing gives the existing rows a value, so the statement cannot succeed on a table with rows. The db-skills bad example runs ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN sku text NOT NULL on the 100,000-row orders and asserts SQLSTATE 23502 (not_null_violation, "column contains null values"): the migration aborts at deploy time, after taking ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on the table to find that out. It passes on the empty database of the test suite, which is why it reaches review. With a constant default the column is metadata-only: "the default value is evaluated at the time of the statement and the result stored in the table's metadata" ([ALTER TABLE](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altertable.html)); the good example adds status text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'new' and the filenode stays.

 The fix

sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN sku text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'unknown';


Or, when no constant makes sense: add it nullable, backfill in batches, then make it NOT NULL through a validated CHECK (PG007).

 When it does not apply

- The table is created in the same migration set.
- The column has a DEFAULT (a volatile one is PG005), is an identity column, a generated column or a serial: each gives every row a value (identity and generated rewrite the table, PG005).
- The column is a PRIMARY KEY in the same clause: the key fails for other reasons on a non-empty table (PG012 and PG017 apply).",
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/lurielabs/lurie-guard/blob/main/rules/pg/PG006-add-column-not-null-without-default/RULE.md",
              "id": "PG006",
              "name": "add-column-not-null-without-default",
              "properties": {
                "skill": "db-skills/skills/pg-online-migrations",
                "tags": [
                  "postgresql",
                  "migration",
