            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error",
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "\`ALTER TABLE ... ADD [CONSTRAINT name] CHECK (...)\` without \`NOT VALID\`, on a table that is not created in the same migration set.",
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## What it detects

\`ALTER TABLE ... ADD [CONSTRAINT name] CHECK (...)\` without \`NOT VALID\`, on a table that is not created in the same migration set.

## Why it matters

\`ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT\` takes \`ACCESS EXCLUSIVE\` ("an \`ACCESS EXCLUSIVE\` lock is acquired unless explicitly noted", [ALTER TABLE](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altertable.html)), and without \`NOT VALID\` it scans every row under that lock: reads and writes wait for the whole scan. The db-skills good example shows the alternative measured: \`ADD CONSTRAINT ... CHECK (...) NOT VALID\` holds \`AccessExclusiveLock\` only for the catalog change (\`pg_locks\` asserted, commit at once), and \`VALIDATE CONSTRAINT\` then runs under \`SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE\`, which does not block reads or writes. The pg-keys-and-types good example swaps a \`CHECK\` with \`NOT VALID\` then \`VALIDATE\` on a live table and asserts that the table's filenode does not change.

## The fix

\`\`\`sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT orders_amount_check CHECK (amount >= 0) NOT VALID;
-- separate transaction (a separate migration where the tool wraps files):
ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT orders_amount_check;
\`\`\`

## When it does not apply

- The table is created in the same migration set.
- The constraint already says \`NOT VALID\` (PG010 then checks that the \`VALIDATE\` is not in the same transaction).
- A \`CHECK\` inside \`ADD COLUMN\` (a column constraint on a new column) is not this statement shape.",
                "text": " What it detects

ALTER TABLE ... ADD [CONSTRAINT name] CHECK (...) without NOT VALID, on a table that is not created in the same migration set.

 Why it matters

ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT takes ACCESS EXCLUSIVE ("an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock is acquired unless explicitly noted", [ALTER TABLE](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altertable.html)), and without NOT VALID it scans every row under that lock: reads and writes wait for the whole scan. The db-skills good example shows the alternative measured: ADD CONSTRAINT ... CHECK (...) NOT VALID holds AccessExclusiveLock only for the catalog change (pg_locks asserted, commit at once), and VALIDATE CONSTRAINT then runs under SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE, which does not block reads or writes. The pg-keys-and-types good example swaps a CHECK with NOT VALID then VALIDATE on a live table and asserts that the table's filenode does not change.

 The fix

sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT orders_amount_check CHECK (amount = 0) NOT VALID;
-- separate transaction (a separate migration where the tool wraps files):
ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT orders_amount_check;


 When it does not apply

- The table is created in the same migration set.
- The constraint already says NOT VALID (PG010 then checks that the VALIDATE is not in the same transaction).
- A CHECK inside ADD COLUMN (a column constraint on a new column) is not this statement shape.",
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/lurielabs/lurie-guard/blob/main/rules/pg/PG009-check-without-not-valid/RULE.md",
              "id": "PG009",
              "name": "check-without-not-valid",
              "properties": {
                "skill": "db-skills/skills/pg-online-migrations",
                "tags": [
                  "postgresql",
                  "migration",
                ],
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PG009: \`ADD CHECK\` without \`NOT VALID\`",
              },
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error",
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "\`ALTER TABLE ... VALIDATE CONSTRAINT name\` in the same transaction as the \`ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT name ... NOT VALID\` that created it: inside the same explicit \`BEGIN\` block, or in the same file when the framework wraps the file in one transaction (Flyway, Liquibase, Rails, Django, Alembic, Prisma, dbmate, Sqitch, or \`transaction: always\`).",
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## What it detects

\`ALTER TABLE ... VALIDATE CONSTRAINT name\` in the same transaction as the \`ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT name ... NOT VALID\` that created it: inside the same explicit \`BEGIN\` block, or in the same file when the framework wraps the file in one transaction (Flyway, Liquibase, Rails, Django, Alembic, Prisma, dbmate, Sqitch, or \`transaction: always\`).

## Why it matters

Locks are "held until the end of the transaction" ([explicit locking](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/explicit-locking.html)). \`ADD FOREIGN KEY ... NOT VALID\` takes \`SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE\` on both tables and \`ADD CHECK ... NOT VALID\` takes \`ACCESS EXCLUSIVE\`; when \`VALIDATE\` runs in the same transaction, its full scan happens while that lock is still held, which is exactly the one-step foreign key the db-skills bad example measures: a scan of \`orders\` under the lock and an \`INSERT INTO accounts\` from another session failing with \`55P03\`. The good example commits between the two steps and asserts that \`VALIDATE\` holds only \`ShareUpdateExclusiveLock\` on \`orders\` and \`RowShareLock\` on \`accounts\`, so writes continue.

## The fix

\`\`\`sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT orders_account_id_fkey
  FOREIGN KEY (account_id) REFERENCES accounts (id) NOT VALID;
COMMIT;
BEGIN;
ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT orders_account_id_fkey;
\`\`\`

Where the framework wraps each file in a transaction, put the \`VALIDATE\` in the next migration file, or use the framework's opt-out so the file runs statement by statement.

## When it does not apply

- The \`ADD ... NOT VALID\` and the \`VALIDATE\` are in different transactions: different files, or a \`COMMIT\` between them in a file that is not wrapped.
- The table is created in the same migration set.
- The constraint being validated was not added \`NOT VALID\` in this file (the detector only knows constraints it saw).",
                "text": " What it detects

ALTER TABLE ... VALIDATE CONSTRAINT name in the same transaction as the ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT name ... NOT VALID that created it: inside the same explicit BEGIN block, or in the same file when the framework wraps the file in one transaction (Flyway, Liquibase, Rails, Django, Alembic, Prisma, dbmate, Sqitch, or transaction: always).

 Why it matters

Locks are "held until the end of the transaction" ([explicit locking](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/explicit-locking.html)). ADD FOREIGN KEY ... NOT VALID takes SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE on both tables and ADD CHECK ... NOT VALID takes ACCESS EXCLUSIVE; when VALIDATE runs in the same transaction, its full scan happens while that lock is still held, which is exactly the one-step foreign key the db-skills bad example measures: a scan of orders under the lock and an INSERT INTO accounts from another session failing with 55P03. The good example commits between the two steps and asserts that VALIDATE holds only ShareUpdateExclusiveLock on orders and RowShareLock on accounts, so writes continue.

 The fix

sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT orders_account_id_fkey
  FOREIGN KEY (account_id) REFERENCES accounts (id) NOT VALID;
COMMIT;
BEGIN;
ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT orders_account_id_fkey;


Where the framework wraps each file in a transaction, put the VALIDATE in the next migration file, or use the framework's opt-out so the file runs statement by statement.

 When it does not apply

- The ADD ... NOT VALID and the VALIDATE are in different transactions: different files, or a COMMIT between them in a file that is not wrapped.
- The table is created in the same migration set.
- The constraint being validated was not added NOT VALID in this file (the detector only knows constraints it saw).",
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/lurielabs/lurie-guard/blob/main/rules/pg/PG010-validate-in-same-transaction/RULE.md",
              "id": "PG010",
              "name": "validate-in-same-transaction",
              "properties": {
                "skill": "db-skills/skills/pg-online-migrations",
                "tags": [
                  "postgresql",
                  "migration",
                ],
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PG010: \`VALIDATE CONSTRAINT\` in the same transaction as the \`NOT VALID\` add",
              },
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning",
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "\`ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE\` on a table that is not created in the same migration set. The detector sees only the new type, not the column's current type, so it reports every retype of an existing table and names the binary-coercible cases in the message.",
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## What it detects

\`ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE\` on a table that is not created in the same migration set. The detector sees only the new type, not the column's current type, so it reports every retype of an existing table and names the binary-coercible cases in the message.

## Why it matters

A type change "will normally cause the entire table and its indexes to be rewritten", unless the old type is binary coercible to the new one and there is no \`USING\` clause ([ALTER TABLE](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altertable.html)); rebuilds "temporarily require as much as double the disk space". The db-skills bad examples assert it twice with \`pg_relation_filenode\`: \`int\` to \`bigint\` on the 100,000-row \`orders\`, and \`varchar(20)\` to \`varchar(10)\` on \`customers\`, both write a new copy of the table under \`ACCESS EXCLUSIVE\`, which blocks reads and writes for the whole copy.

The severity is \`warning\` in the MVP, not \`error\`, although the cost is measured: the detector sees only the new type, and a binary-coercible retype (\`varchar(50)\` to \`text\`) is a safe change that the rule cannot tell apart from a rewrite without a schema snapshot. An \`error\` must never fire on a safe change ([RULES.md](../../../docs/RULES.md), "False positives"). The rule goes back to \`error\` once a schema snapshot input exists ([ROADMAP.md](../../../docs/ROADMAP.md)). The cost fixture (\`fixtures/cost/test.sql\`) stays and keeps running.

## The fix

Expand/contract across releases:

\`\`\`sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN account_id_new bigint;
-- dual-write account_id and account_id_new from the application; backfill in batches
-- switch reads to account_id_new; then, in a later release:
ALTER TABLE orders DROP COLUMN account_id;
ALTER TABLE orders RENAME COLUMN account_id_new TO account_id;
\`\`\`

## When it does not apply

- The table is created in the same migration set.
- The current type is binary-coercible to the new one: \`varchar(n)\` to a larger or unbounded \`varchar\` or to \`text\`, \`numeric(p,s)\` to a larger \`p\` with the same \`s\`, \`timestamp(p)\` to a larger precision. The detector cannot see the current type from the diff, so these are reported; suppress with a reason that names the current type (\`-- lurie-guard: ignore PG011 reason="varchar(50) to varchar(200), binary-coercible"\`).
- A small lookup table that fits in a few pages: the rewrite takes milliseconds; suppress with a reason, and keep \`lock_timeout\` set (PG004).",
                "text": " What it detects

ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE on a table that is not created in the same migration set. The detector sees only the new type, not the column's current type, so it reports every retype of an existing table and names the binary-coercible cases in the message.

 Why it matters

A type change "will normally cause the entire table and its indexes to be rewritten", unless the old type is binary coercible to the new one and there is no USING clause ([ALTER TABLE](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altertable.html)); rebuilds "temporarily require as much as double the disk space". The db-skills bad examples assert it twice with pg_relation_filenode: int to bigint on the 100,000-row orders, and varchar(20) to varchar(10) on customers, both write a new copy of the table under ACCESS EXCLUSIVE, which blocks reads and writes for the whole copy.

The severity is warning in the MVP, not error, although the cost is measured: the detector sees only the new type, and a binary-coercible retype (varchar(50) to text) is a safe change that the rule cannot tell apart from a rewrite without a schema snapshot. An error must never fire on a safe change ([RULES.md](../../../docs/RULES.md), "False positives"). The rule goes back to error once a schema snapshot input exists ([ROADMAP.md](../../../docs/ROADMAP.md)). The cost fixture (fixtures/cost/test.sql) stays and keeps running.

 The fix

Expand/contract across releases:

sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN account_id_new bigint;
-- dual-write account_id and account_id_new from the application; backfill in batches
-- switch reads to account_id_new; then, in a later release:
ALTER TABLE orders DROP COLUMN account_id;
ALTER TABLE orders RENAME COLUMN account_id_new TO account_id;


 When it does not apply

- The table is created in the same migration set.
- The current type is binary-coercible to the new one: varchar(n) to a larger or unbounded varchar or to text, numeric(p,s) to a larger p with the same s, timestamp(p) to a larger precision. The detector cannot see the current type from the diff, so these are reported; suppress with a reason that names the current type (-- lurie-guard: ignore PG011 reason="varchar(50) to varchar(200), binary-coercible").
- A small lookup table that fits in a few pages: the rewrite takes milliseconds; suppress with a reason, and keep lock_timeout set (PG004).",
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/lurielabs/lurie-guard/blob/main/rules/pg/PG011-alter-column-type-rewrites/RULE.md",
              "id": "PG011",
              "name": "alter-column-type-rewrites",
              "properties": {
                "skill": "db-skills/skills/pg-online-migrations",
