exports[`review > plans one review with suggestions, hides fingerprints, respects max_comments and the diff 1`] = `
{
  "body": "**lurie-guard**: 8 findings (4 errors, 3 warnings, 1 info).
Report-only: this check passes whatever it finds. To block merges on errors, add \`fail_on: error\` to \`.lurie-guard.yml\` on the base branch.

Suppressed with a reason:
- \`migrations/0001_orders.sql:2\` PG004: the runner sets lock_timeout through ALTER ROLE

<!-- lurie-guard:fingerprints 0526c5c547c39fe1259ed8ea4d7ea111,64732e7ab494e2e960586bec099893fc,697b241ab30e66458bcf67681a63dc5c,98f939eb984677b6b159c9822df6e280,9bc31fc1b14f87db8c9258034f2df3ea,a49c8c5368b691f1ad26c261bca782cc,c7f21f6386c0fe5e9120a0454fa99c57,d56ec5cc4fa760eb03f483e44f5993a5 -->",
  "comments": [
    {
      "body": "**PG003** (error): \`CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY\` cannot run inside a transaction block: PostgreSQL rejects it with SQLSTATE \`25001\` and the migration fails. This statement runs in a file rails wraps in a transaction. Fix: Rails: call \`disable_ddl_transaction!\` in the migration class. No exception: the statement never works inside a transaction. (PG003, [evidence](https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-online-migrations/examples/good/test.sql#L129-L131))

<!-- lurie-guard:fp:64732e7ab494e2e960586bec099893fc -->",
      "line": 3,
      "path": "db/migrate/20261019120000_add_index.rb",
      "side": "RIGHT",
    },
    {
      "body": "**PG006** (error): \`ADD COLUMN sku ... NOT NULL\` without a default fails on any table that has rows: nothing fills the existing rows of \`orders\`, so PostgreSQL rejects the statement with SQLSTATE \`23502\` and the migration aborts. Add it with a constant default (\`NOT NULL DEFAULT <constant>\`: metadata-only, no rewrite), or add it nullable, backfill in batches, then \`SET NOT NULL\` through a validated \`CHECK\` (PG007). Not needed if \`orders\` is new in this release or empty. (PG006, [evidence](https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-online-migrations/examples/bad/test.sql#L117-L123))

\`\`\`sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN sku text NOT NULL DEFAULT <constant>;
-- or: add it nullable, backfill in batches, then the validated-CHECK path (PG007)
\`\`\`

<!-- lurie-guard:fp:98f939eb984677b6b159c9822df6e280 -->",
      "line": 2,
      "path": "migrations/0001_orders.sql",
      "side": "RIGHT",
    },
    {
      "body": "**PG001** (error): \`CREATE INDEX\` holds a \`SHARE\` lock on \`orders\` for the whole build; every insert, update and delete on \`orders\` waits. Build it with \`CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_created_at_idx ON orders (created_at)\`, outside a transaction (PG003 checks that). Not needed if \`orders\` is new in this release or empty. (PG001, [evidence](https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-online-migrations/examples/bad/test.sql#L99-L104))

\`\`\`suggestion
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_created_at_idx ON orders (created_at);
\`\`\`

<!-- lurie-guard:fp:0526c5c547c39fe1259ed8ea4d7ea111 -->",
      "line": 3,
      "path": "migrations/0001_orders.sql",
      "side": "RIGHT",
    },
    {
      "body": "**PG004** (warning): \`ALTER TABLE\` waits for a heavy lock (\`ACCESS EXCLUSIVE\` or \`SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE\`) on \`orders\` with \`lock_timeout\` at its default of 0 (wait forever): it queues behind any long transaction, and every query that arrives after it queues behind the DDL. Run \`SET lock_timeout = '2s'\` (or \`SET LOCAL\` inside the transaction) before the first DDL and retry on SQLSTATE \`55P03\`. Not needed when your migration runner sets \`lock_timeout\` for every session: say so with \`lock_timeout_set_by_runner: true\` in \`.lurie-guard.yml\`. (PG004, [evidence](https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-online-migrations/examples/bad/test.sql#L24-L49))

\`\`\`sql
SET lock_timeout = '2s';  -- a few seconds; retry the step on 55P03
ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT orders_account_id_fkey
  FOREIGN KEY (account_id) REFERENCES accounts (id);
\`\`\`

<!-- lurie-guard:fp:697b241ab30e66458bcf67681a63dc5c -->",
      "line": 5,
      "path": "migrations/0001_orders.sql",
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "start_line": 4,
      "start_side": "RIGHT",
    },
    {
      "body": "**PG008** (error): \`ADD FOREIGN KEY\` validates every row of \`orders\` while it holds \`SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE\` on \`orders\` and on \`accounts\`: writes on both tables wait for the whole scan. Add it with \`NOT VALID\` (catalog only), then \`ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT orders_account_id_fkey\` in a separate transaction, which takes only \`SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE\` and lets writes continue. Not needed if \`orders\` is new in this release or empty. (PG008, [evidence](https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-online-migrations/examples/bad/test.sql#L69-L85))

\`\`\`suggestion
ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT orders_account_id_fkey
  FOREIGN KEY (account_id) REFERENCES accounts (id) NOT VALID;
\`\`\`

<!-- lurie-guard:fp:c7f21f6386c0fe5e9120a0454fa99c57 -->",
      "line": 5,
      "path": "migrations/0001_orders.sql",
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "start_line": 4,
      "start_side": "RIGHT",
    },
    {
      "body": "**PG017** (warning): \`id serial PRIMARY KEY\` on \`customers\` is a 4-byte key with a separate sequence: it stops at 2147483647 (inserts then fail with SQLSTATE \`2200H\`), and moving to \`bigint\` later rewrites the whole table. Declare it \`id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY\`: 4 more bytes per row, never runs out, and the SQL-standard identity refuses hand-written ids. Not needed for a small lookup table that cannot reach 2 billion rows; suppress with that reason. (PG017, [evidence](https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-keys-and-types/examples/bad/test.sql#L29-L37))

\`\`\`suggestion
CREATE TABLE customers (
  id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  email varchar(20),
  created_at timestamp
);
\`\`\`

<!-- lurie-guard:fp:d56ec5cc4fa760eb03f483e44f5993a5 -->",
      "line": 10,
      "path": "migrations/0001_orders.sql",
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "start_line": 6,
      "start_side": "RIGHT",
    },
    {
      "body": "**PG021** (info): \`email varchar(20)\` on \`customers\` enforces only a maximum: it still accepts \`''\`, an explicit cast silently truncates to 20 characters, and shrinking the limit later rewrites the table. Declare it \`text\` with a named check, \`CONSTRAINT customers_email_len CHECK (char_length(email) BETWEEN 1 AND 20)\`, which states the real rule and can be changed with \`NOT VALID\` then \`VALIDATE\` and no rewrite. Not needed when the limit is a real business rule the team keeps deliberately, or for fixed-length codes from a standard (then prefer \`text\` with a regular-expression check). (PG021, [evidence](https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-keys-and-types/examples/bad/test.sql#L39-L52))

\`\`\`sql
email text,
CONSTRAINT customers_email_len CHECK (char_length(email) BETWEEN 1 AND 20)
\`\`\`

<!-- lurie-guard:fp:a49c8c5368b691f1ad26c261bca782cc -->",
      "line": 8,
      "path": "migrations/0001_orders.sql",
      "side": "RIGHT",
    },
    {
      "body": "**PG019** (warning): \`created_at timestamp\` on \`customers\` is a timestamp without time zone: PostgreSQL "silently ignore[s] any time zone indication" on input, so the same instant written from two sessions with different \`TimeZone\` settings is stored as two values (12 hours apart in the db-skills test). Declare it \`timestamptz\`: stored as UTC, same 8 bytes, converted on output to the session's zone. Not needed for a wall-clock value that is deliberately zone-free (a store's opening time, a recurring local alarm); suppress with that reason. (PG019, [evidence](https://github.com/rafapeixer/db-skills/blob/c6eb44e9ab3b9458099b0a808ca4a2cde9f2db57/skills/pg-keys-and-types/examples/bad/test.sql#L54-L61))

\`\`\`suggestion
CREATE TABLE customers (
  id serial PRIMARY KEY,
  email varchar(20),
  created_at timestamptz
);
\`\`\`

<!-- lurie-guard:fp:9bc31fc1b14f87db8c9258034f2df3ea -->",
      "line": 10,
      "path": "migrations/0001_orders.sql",
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "start_line": 6,
      "start_side": "RIGHT",
    },
  ],
}
`;

